[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8d1fdb-1e8d-5f02-9ea7-c3c298ae432f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361248} {:answer/id #uuid "af862e42-0cba-5667-86e1-bcba2137990c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361614} {:answer/id #uuid "c253a7fe-6f5f-512c-aff1-bf5aa8dd058e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361708} {:answer/id #uuid "ed37a445-de09-58c0-b587-33b13984c10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfef024-1b6c-555f-8a26-33bf987ce229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360947} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9e95f8-2048-5fb9-b5b3-a7a136bdb4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a53a3d-9f1a-5b06-bcde-4575632a0617" :question/math-academy-id "q-98665" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397fefec-25e9-51df-956e-4d4b55444186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361098} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea68c89-9002-5f07-8a7c-b297b7adc902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361395} {:answer/id #uuid "c10febe1-3c66-57e0-b62d-808330b25e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361702} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} {:answer/id #uuid "d318e5b2-6b3f-531d-a4fc-c635478aeb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aff563-951e-5537-9949-923b00386848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361732} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef995365-0b4c-502d-9aad-9595d82e6b2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5692f221-3e0b-5059-a8e0-07dee6b9fc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98708" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=4$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\sqrt{t-3},t-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(4)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(4)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t-3}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}},1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(4) & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t-3}}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(t-3)^{3}}},0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(4)$ and ${r}^{″}(4)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4) & =⟨2,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(4) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(4){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(4)\\times {r}^{″}(4)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=5\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f5699-9400-5499-a42f-6b0b1436ca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360836} {:answer/id #uuid "16a08b96-db20-5af1-bb77-ce8bbea3de6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360930} {:answer/id #uuid "7304f18f-2868-5d0e-8676-adea6e556791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361342} {:answer/id #uuid "8773e205-1259-5af9-a9ce-98084fdaf7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4},\\,\\frac{3}{{t}^{3}}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361440} {:answer/id #uuid "9566393f-9e21-58f0-94b6-2f87781b816e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,-\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16a08b96-db20-5af1-bb77-ce8bbea3de6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360930} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf601c3f-b54f-5e15-9831-b80626b06474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b1c2371-6e4d-55bf-8462-352cb3ad4deb" :question/math-academy-id "q-99264" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\text{ }5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2089f74f-7bb1-531f-9f54-4e2969f07e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360974} {:answer/id #uuid "54599985-5dd3-502a-b9ef-88b1d618a930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361211} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} {:answer/id #uuid "72ddff39-ee62-599f-a890-d26c22490faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361340} {:answer/id #uuid "93d239b8-90ad-5454-b0f7-400662e44c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b1f0e-03d5-557f-83d3-fc825eebaa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03caba5-0350-5e9d-aa7c-ebd7a638e046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25941eb3-76ad-51ee-8456-c5400a4c8aea" :question/math-academy-id "q-9944" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{2}{sin}^{3}⁡x$ at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={sin}^{3}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)({sin}^{3}⁡x)+({x}^{2})(3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{sin}^{3}⁡x+3{x}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=0} & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡(0)+3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679ec2c6-4cf0-58a9-8a21-1f4d39731ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361303} {:answer/id #uuid "a52a1d16-23ef-5d49-9472-952fff86d524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361576} {:answer/id #uuid "a619762f-caa2-52e1-b601-2b9e273c0987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361580} {:answer/id #uuid "a87617f5-39f9-51e8-b406-1285e46df49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361591} {:answer/id #uuid "f325c81a-7aa9-55a1-a5eb-40242c496d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87617f5-39f9-51e8-b406-1285e46df49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae7d53d-2d7c-5f3d-9b5c-661fa3f825e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def34be0-5343-5a7a-b63c-7b336175d666" :question/math-academy-id "q-99505" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0f9cf17608e32b91f4efbb243702286b484938c015f74f0b59ca906dc24c593a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b32cee-241d-5209-b781-42dafeabc88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361055} {:answer/id #uuid "60d241f0-b87c-544c-8f1d-20a96cc18fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361272} {:answer/id #uuid "9d616058-6541-54d6-9e7a-df268c360a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361543} {:answer/id #uuid "b04fe489-172a-5204-8bbb-e255bb7ba816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361618} {:answer/id #uuid "d272c1f6-cc10-5e2d-89b8-ea470069df61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b32cee-241d-5209-b781-42dafeabc88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361055} :answer-field/id #uuid "f592b5e3-05d1-5d7c-a4b5-9761e364d136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5d93a21-4b46-5cff-b9c3-bf3f9cd7e0d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99507" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-9\\cdot {2}^{-x}) & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({2}^{-1})^{x} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d3477662fd9a07c6f1543728a1562f4fed62f91350741078a7d568f500d32d0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}=-9\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e35960-a20c-5092-afee-6e55a2ae5eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360932} {:answer/id #uuid "287f4560-439a-5562-9bf1-4554ba564558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361013} {:answer/id #uuid "378780f8-763e-554c-afe8-65b91f041472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361089} {:answer/id #uuid "5839514a-0aae-5a5b-bd89-80dfe8dd9082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361238} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e7ef57-9f7d-5478-b810-3931fa423334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5839514a-0aae-5a5b-bd89-80dfe8dd9082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361238} :answer-field/id #uuid "c249ff05-e298-57e8-a69b-dd52212a3ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4d1e60-e5ad-568f-b928-a3604e33809e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99653" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{8{e}^{4t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(-2{e}^{2t})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(4{e}^{4t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨1,-2{e}^{2t},0⟩}{(\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{4{e}^{4t}+1})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{2t},1,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4{e}^{4t}+1}}⟨1,\\,-2{e}^{2t},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-4{e}^{4t}-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4t}+1}⟨0,0,-(4{e}^{4t}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125dc459-3647-5955-b51e-d821d2de7c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360908} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f8943-1000-5d36-ac6e-ddc10b59128f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} {:answer/id #uuid "42f0da77-ebb0-5e59-93e1-609dad760bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361131} {:answer/id #uuid "803b692b-37d0-54e8-9040-d728e525e6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361408} {:answer/id #uuid "d919e4d9-a221-5ad6-99c6-1feacd68593c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f8943-1000-5d36-ac6e-ddc10b59128f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2fb855-21fa-5953-a702-7baf5486c1c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10464afa-e24d-5bb8-8ed8-ff334320cdee" :question/math-academy-id "q-99724" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdce64bf5d02850bbedc2b89af3b2e7447b733831c09d1955e4a9ee97e82d556.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Hence, $c=2.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2 & =4 \\\\ k+2 & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c6c8cf-4f52-5e19-a6a0-f83a65ac7c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360854} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f97b0-83e6-564c-b5dc-769853af4472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361452} {:answer/id #uuid "b973b826-6371-5d1b-9cf5-4fbc6c3551b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361664} {:answer/id #uuid "c4634de2-da5c-55c6-aef1-e625b6a8efec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361721} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a4b955-59cf-5672-b582-f4d572b0d22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b8f97b0-83e6-564c-b5dc-769853af4472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361452} :answer-field/id #uuid "77178a41-d25c-5f86-93be-403ec472bfe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da377ea7-a436-56fd-bc10-dd4b8618d147" :question/math-academy-id "q-9978" :question/problem "![](images/79/79889a1366311f6175a3cc736d5cd800a19a9d843daffc824d36c0690f0e3310.png)\n\nFind the area of the closed region between the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =4(2-(-2))-(\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{8}{3})) \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961a3bee-0aa7-5b3a-9490-64e96f97c0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469990} {:answer/id #uuid "a379ae5f-f528-5be2-9828-0f082c70b0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a379ae5f-f528-5be2-9828-0f082c70b0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469991} :answer-field/id #uuid "9695d404-e5b5-5119-b269-1365e2848754" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1131ba72-54f0-5869-80e4-5124768441a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469992} {:answer/id #uuid "974321a3-4afd-5560-9447-d00b39e3e328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1131ba72-54f0-5869-80e4-5124768441a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469992} :answer-field/id #uuid "43b6849a-4395-57b5-9a47-f34aef60036a" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470608} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95b5dec-0d95-5892-99cd-3d44ced67f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469994} {:answer/id #uuid "90fba07c-0d7f-54bf-a9de-e9e4d0c05bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d95b5dec-0d95-5892-99cd-3d44ced67f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469994} :answer-field/id #uuid "cec511de-6ce2-5c26-905e-fb28356ffd0f" :answer-field/key "$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-99815" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72eb4719-e98f-5cb9-a9e4-ea5ac615be39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470014} {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b831ddfd-2e75-57a3-803c-fce7fe0f20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470015} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a5031a-4e64-5a88-9ff5-3c50b425c755" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61342765-a723-57af-9b7c-1e66b2684508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470016} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fd08386-f78d-5999-af5f-11dcacc7153d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470017} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbf0f0e-0c01-528a-9359-099c9f7fa8e3" :answer-field/key "${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfceee01-94dd-5b9c-9d9e-e30c9256d930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470018} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8d57c-bf1b-5f00-8da9-25440a3756bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4425320b-ac0f-5887-8acf-749452911dc2" :answer-field/key "$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8889f645-9334-518f-83dc-7064dd4f1d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product” is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product”; “$S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} {:answer/id #uuid "344eb24e-1156-549a-aa63-f7b70c503bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362605} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa8cd0a-d89b-5bf5-ac6c-3d535195e448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362606} {:answer/id #uuid "be65387c-8a7a-5830-b531-fb3b8cd8edc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362607} {:answer/id #uuid "532d3b94-1153-561c-80f6-cf5aaf59db52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec63c256-7f17-50ef-ab3a-8b079a43b2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cccbf905-9f63-54b3-9f8d-d6f0dbce85ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-106251" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,-1)-f(0,2,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨0,2,1⟩) \\\\ 2-(-7) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-4y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{y-2}{2}=-\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,\\,y=2-2t,\\,z=1-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t)+8(2-2t)-4(1-2t) & =9 \\\\ 2t+16-16t-4+8t & =9 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2-2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-2(\\frac{1}{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1179be5b-67ba-56fc-be5e-2e65b68e77b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{2}-3)\\sqrt{1+({y}^{3}-9y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362670} {:answer/id #uuid "f388400a-0796-5b61-b9d0-2c9d4478df7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362671} {:answer/id #uuid "70a704a4-76e3-5ec1-a22d-84cc5c20e56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{2}-3)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362672} {:answer/id #uuid "d3baf482-0028-52b4-944f-f098ffcecdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362673} {:answer/id #uuid "86552aaf-d17e-5107-a376-d4e2a69dcd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86552aaf-d17e-5107-a376-d4e2a69dcd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362674} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0023f43-5370-5144-93ca-c1ebad284580" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e87f452-4fb9-5bde-b89d-3b3509400f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109256" :question/problem "![](images/22/22328ca144ecfaf7c1d52abd77c59f03dbf3c0da36fff21a809ce4e456ed56a5.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x={y}^{3}-9y$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-9y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-3$ to $y=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-9y)=3{y}^{2}-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2}-9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}({y}^{3}-9y)\\sqrt{1+9({y}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364fe6cb-9d03-53ce-a165-42f5c54b95ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362869} {:answer/id #uuid "c21264c7-8089-52ce-9a48-6dad38c7f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362870} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9db4ec-3467-518d-a30c-e4d04ca7e957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362871} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b710cd-dff5-5c54-9db5-d82e41461d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362872} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe8a21-d6a2-507f-9611-cdd51fb00208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe8a21-d6a2-507f-9611-cdd51fb00208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873} :answer-field/id #uuid "08dd7829-9567-51b3-9a69-757f02b76b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65eb7d35-cf19-5d83-87df-2f6cd4fcb07e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114317" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-{\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4(1/4)}-0\\cdot {e}^{4\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{16}[{e}^{4(1/4)}-{e}^{4\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{e}{4}-0]-\\frac{1}{16}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}-\\frac{e}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be70cf20-a712-5709-b35b-937dc2c0d369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362889} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6ddf74-4770-595f-899b-116441f857f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362890} {:answer/id #uuid "15446dd9-5015-5403-b01a-d1888f4b4aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362891} {:answer/id #uuid "02430b8b-e95e-5e00-bf36-982ce578dff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362892} {:answer/id #uuid "98bb0d83-f17c-55b7-8962-d5f1322270b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98bb0d83-f17c-55b7-8962-d5f1322270b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a37bc31-d8c2-5721-a783-ab38279822c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f9dc4af-4f4e-5627-b6ea-707abe485f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115593" :question/problem "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3978ab12-c2c3-556b-8b3e-3e2439bb98af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} {:answer/id #uuid "921abcc9-664f-5748-a32a-b8cb3a3a1679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362992} {:answer/id #uuid "55926885-53a5-5ed5-b872-7944d1c6177d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362993} {:answer/id #uuid "87181d2e-ae8e-5568-bad0-b0d06ff451c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362994} {:answer/id #uuid "70de9d14-e9f3-5545-9a7f-d37446be4ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3978ab12-c2c3-556b-8b3e-3e2439bb98af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c54a12c4-ac53-57bf-ac59-6e5331dff070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05fbe250-8eb4-5b81-83cc-08a815c1aec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123510" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{u}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9775c1a1-be1f-5d4a-8b38-2b22b3877645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+5)}{3}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363005} {:answer/id #uuid "25af2b05-29e6-557b-85e3-77fd7abe9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363006} {:answer/id #uuid "91cb4a25-ae24-57be-8930-89439bfcd808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7d50e-bf4c-5d3c-8f65-093e0d804de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363008} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c244ca-ce7e-5f14-8b4e-fc2f90fec15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cb4a25-ae24-57be-8930-89439bfcd808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5a6fdb-0204-5d6b-854a-5929492322cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d889fae-ff07-5704-acc9-b1fed02f79be" :question/math-academy-id "q-12565" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dacd-9221-5bef-a3e2-054140ce493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} {:answer/id #uuid "9d49b809-9260-5d2e-bf3c-edbf1cfbe326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363041} {:answer/id #uuid "311f4edb-1ed9-5bdd-a81b-ea812b499a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363042} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf0b43a-7db8-50c5-a40c-5a36e706ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363043} {:answer/id #uuid "857a50bb-2584-5d08-9f49-c2a69907123b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dacd-9221-5bef-a3e2-054140ce493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363040} :answer-field/id #uuid "438741cb-1bf1-5078-92c8-7fcd9f079cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a136203-dd8c-5077-8693-b3c3a213b678" :question/math-academy-id "q-130248" :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\frac{1}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}={x}^{2},{f}_{2}=3x,{f}_{1}=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{x})+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+3x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42791993-497f-5d8d-b8b1-76a486145b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363068} {:answer/id #uuid "5275ebef-196c-55ed-b7be-368e39313e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363069} {:answer/id #uuid "19d964b3-4d82-5a07-8d95-87fb890d5233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363070} {:answer/id #uuid "5b459fcd-4150-51bf-8cf3-5a499ccae7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363071} {:answer/id #uuid "81dea80a-5bdb-5f50-af91-2aae2e0c71d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5275ebef-196c-55ed-b7be-368e39313e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363069} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cfe9af0-6727-55bc-a035-1c1e1d5b43e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3beac47b-9bf4-555f-b72b-5aef346b5fc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131028" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨2,1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,1,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ -7-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6x,\\,-2y,\\,-4z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1},\\,-2{c}_{2},\\,-4{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =⟨6{c}_{1},\\,-2{c}_{2},\\,-4{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\ -7 & =12{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}-12{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =y=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $12{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}-12{c}_{3}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(2t)-2t-12(3t) & =-7 \\\\ 24t-2t-36t & =-7 \\\\ -14t & =-7 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =3(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} {:answer/id #uuid "69b89807-97fa-57a7-88d2-88b07ce8c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363178} {:answer/id #uuid "52b9287f-df57-59c5-b415-72f23606c6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363179} {:answer/id #uuid "0c35c334-f400-5dc1-bf8d-276f2e3f2b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363180} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c82834-ccb7-5845-b945-d22abbba57eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d66d8c-c095-55c5-82e5-156fb6a77fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53a1aa4-cef0-536f-a0a5-781c11e47a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "337abe4f-5c56-58ff-ad12-408abbd32afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140078" :question/problem "Given the transformation\n $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y \\\\ 4z-2x \\\\ z-4x-3y\\end{bmatrix},$ find its rank." :question/worked-solution "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef4f44e-6d79-526b-a93e-f229a1ed3dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{-2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363199} {:answer/id #uuid "be75fdd8-49c3-5720-aa14-5bf6b23f6f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363200} {:answer/id #uuid "f288e8dc-d03c-5849-9de6-7f832f534bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363201} {:answer/id #uuid "312d113a-1309-533b-bf4f-3d3a3da1e08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d479be-872e-5b2b-9a6f-55bc9a392083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363198} :answer-field/id #uuid "43da5e18-1a97-5649-82f0-f3809dd899d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf5de7bd-3234-5d41-a9a5-c12fb9a085ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-140841" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=12.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{2}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{5})}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{30} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{15}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4999140-0d13-559f-82aa-04866874811c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363247} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e430b-d923-5296-a7ca-989631c7e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363248} {:answer/id #uuid "80729c9c-5883-5ad9-9c8c-9b30b4df7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363249} {:answer/id #uuid "2077f734-6970-5b4d-a64f-8002e8ee0701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363250} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd952fb-a20a-50c5-b031-7432fb3653ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4999140-0d13-559f-82aa-04866874811c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363247} :answer-field/id #uuid "37e1e6b6-7d76-5dc6-b274-7c6f617f4cd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "753f9054-4350-51a9-85ca-8852ab3b386a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142874" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(Y=2)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(2) & =f(1,2)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{Y}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{2}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{Y}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3aeb14-8104-592d-8036-4fef6627d0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363261} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6f2979-a525-5080-8d4a-ae06fb5aa369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363262} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6b725-d8e2-5b90-be9c-4bbb306f9451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363263} {:answer/id #uuid "04998ebf-6b20-5927-9623-00f607e4bd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363264} {:answer/id #uuid "525aa0b5-ba10-5d07-bfd0-db506a9c107a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f6f2979-a525-5080-8d4a-ae06fb5aa369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363262} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c14e381-71fd-541f-800b-d1d42f6cbd47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5ea1e1a-abf9-5242-bf8e-b7a85e702e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142880" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required conditional probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=6)}{P(Y=6)}=\\frac{f(1,6)}{{f}_{Y}(6)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(6) & =f(1,6)+f(3,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(1,6)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{Y}(6)=\\frac{3}{8}$ and $f(1,6)=\\frac{1}{4}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=6) & =\\frac{f(1,6)}{{f}_{Y}(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fdd98ea-b686-5ea5-abeb-750baf12e325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363268} {:answer/id #uuid "0b99296f-9b99-5d16-ba2b-33fc48e931fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363269} {:answer/id #uuid "b242d10a-3ce8-50f7-a9f3-0b2a1e77a9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363270} {:answer/id #uuid "0277c693-0186-5a04-bbbd-f9ba66564dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363271} {:answer/id #uuid "6185cf3a-cf54-5e7d-a188-ee46f31f9a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b242d10a-3ce8-50f7-a9f3-0b2a1e77a9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363270} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede214a2-474b-58ec-ad6d-adef5ac219de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b16c08e-86de-5a8d-a678-6c00b4090d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142881" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nCompute $P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "By the multiplication law for conditional probability, the required probability is given by\n\n$$\nP(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3)=\\frac{P(X=3,Y=2)}{P(X=3)}=\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(3) & =f(3,2)+f(3,4)+f(3,6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, we have that $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{8}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting ${f}_{X}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $f(3,2)=\\frac{1}{8}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=2\\,|\\,X=3) & =\\frac{f(3,2)}{{f}_{X}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b23a91dd-7a01-54db-9c82-1cde58ce273a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363289} {:answer/id #uuid "8145968b-18ea-5743-b1bd-efc55f689163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363290} {:answer/id #uuid "57859f6d-2835-5a6a-af22-856e77c2c686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363291} {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} {:answer/id #uuid "7678ab30-3836-51cd-9e08-1245f5e719d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "771f4df6-0fd2-59bd-b1c4-8a07438c5de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363292} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c83bc5-8c9c-5ba3-9c2d-a22c540d169d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37840033-8bd0-59aa-a8ed-245c50ed8426" :question/math-academy-id "q-142892" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{3}{20}$ | $\\frac{7}{20}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{9}{20}$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4)=P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=1,Y=4)}{P(Y=4)}=\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(4) & =f(1,4)+f(3,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{20}+\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(4)=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $f(1,4)=\\frac{7}{20}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(1\\,|\\,4) & =P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{20})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8c1a66-fe5f-5cac-826b-bae7697518d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363296} {:answer/id #uuid "6412061e-552b-592d-841a-df9232ae0bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363297} {:answer/id #uuid "73f3f322-78a6-521a-acd6-347960587f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363298} {:answer/id #uuid "18d7d7b9-4a1d-54c7-80e3-56017188c4e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363299} {:answer/id #uuid "a59dcb81-2eb6-57e0-aa3f-d0f0becdc50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73f3f322-78a6-521a-acd6-347960587f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363298} :answer-field/id #uuid "40758d66-7919-514c-8265-b8925b3ffff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d06bf4ef-53cd-5e2d-bbaf-861031a3e125" :question/math-academy-id "q-142895" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{8}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4)=P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=3)}{P(X=4)}=\\frac{f(4,3)}{{f}_{X}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(4) & =f(4,1)+f(4,3)+f(4,5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{8}{27}+\\frac{2}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,3)=\\frac{8}{27}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{X}(4)=\\frac{13}{27}$ and $f(4,3)=\\frac{8}{27}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(3\\,|\\,4) & =P(Y=3\\,|\\,X=4) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,3)}{{f}_{X}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{27})}{(\\frac{13}{27})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d146954b-107a-5bf5-b2c2-58b2d76d2f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363317} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce85799-ee97-543b-b79a-08bcfeb894e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363318} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d3ebc3-d64d-5e87-9163-151fc4e5359e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363319} {:answer/id #uuid "cf3c3657-5609-567b-9359-aff272cdd30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363320} {:answer/id #uuid "4676d494-1558-5c75-a0ac-1ffbf28c079b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4676d494-1558-5c75-a0ac-1ffbf28c079b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363321} :answer-field/id #uuid "d59b5ddd-f1f5-574e-9fb1-16006eceeb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13d8d8db-94c8-50ea-a54d-7920bc95617e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142902" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{8}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{27}$ |\n\nCompute ${f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1)$ given that the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the conditional probability\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1)=P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1).\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1)=\\frac{P(X=4,Y=1)}{P(Y=1)}=\\frac{f(4,1)}{{f}_{Y}(1)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(1) & =f(2,1)+f(4,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{27}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given table, $f(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, substituting  ${f}_{Y}(1)=\\frac{8}{27}$ and $f(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}$ in our formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(4\\,|\\,1) & =P(X=4\\,|\\,Y=1) \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4,1)}{{f}_{Y}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{27})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec25039-c0fd-56ca-b984-bd9349c79ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363365} {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd3e2e0-b449-5ae9-bfe5-39ae00b8ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363367} {:answer/id #uuid "827145a1-4259-51ff-8504-aef48c2e2ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363368} {:answer/id #uuid "0a472974-80e2-5c15-a84b-22f9c2f5a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5703c778-a247-5102-af88-f5464e7312d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "929c6f43-55a6-50e3-98c8-0627bab8c6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-149788" :question/problem "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{-\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-{e}^{-\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{-\\theta })}^{2}+{(-{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{-2\\theta }+{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f17b3f-b619-5663-a742-8cd16caad422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363373} {:answer/id #uuid "2f268941-c5e1-5f87-9aff-31416118fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363374} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf36ad-cc0f-5781-9b02-c40c452e845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+12{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363375} {:answer/id #uuid "eac417bd-f5e6-53b0-85c1-fea6d7f50ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+40{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} :answer-field/id #uuid "388c2572-dc24-57d0-9cb1-bc6bf8263984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd418bfc-f7c5-506e-bc72-5623879aa6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149798" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta +60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4+60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b3cfbcd-030f-561c-b99a-84767df108f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363379} {:answer/id #uuid "447169dc-4770-53ba-84cc-4c795e4b9daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363380} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22b9e6-6d50-50a7-93d3-dd76afc0421f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363381} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f487da-8d53-542b-9e0c-5dd951fc18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363382} {:answer/id #uuid "f12f4761-dcf9-53e1-8a69-75caa608fd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f487da-8d53-542b-9e0c-5dd951fc18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d5c6a81-8daf-5bce-856c-eb12feaaff23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86c222b1-ebbd-591c-81fe-2d687175781c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149821" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b95d3a6-7970-587e-a6e2-ec8585c4f125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363529} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfe1be9-0e6a-5fd3-8ee5-e5a47a3788db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363530} {:answer/id #uuid "a60db2dc-ce4b-5a6e-86ff-0c2607f97197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-\\frac{11}{4})$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363531} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab5540f-b9d7-5fc9-ad8a-b4d603e667cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-\\frac{11}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363532} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7eaee-f6f6-5d6d-8308-70adc79d26af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b95d3a6-7970-587e-a6e2-ec8585c4f125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363529} :answer-field/id #uuid "90beb5cf-42f0-5e02-8e07-82f4c8c55f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "193b3ef5-bb74-515e-aa2f-4427c5bb5f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-165044" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-3)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j & =c(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}i+2t\\,j) \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-3)\\,j & =-\\frac{c}{{t}^{2}}i+2ct\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t} &=-\\frac{c}{{t}^{2}} \\\\ {t}^{2}-3 &=2ct\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $-t=c.$ Substituting $c=-t$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =-2{t}^{2} \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 3(t+1)(t-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+({1}^{2}-3)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1489818f-5ce9-545c-881f-dfa313c88d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363536} {:answer/id #uuid "a863d5d6-4817-5d38-861e-294ff12c8daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363537} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac8a95-910b-5dff-900f-302d27cc3e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363538} {:answer/id #uuid "41dd2e89-20f2-5f82-af5e-e4877efb589e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363539} {:answer/id #uuid "b75d956c-9807-5aca-a281-f664d5d8c25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1489818f-5ce9-545c-881f-dfa313c88d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363536} :answer-field/id #uuid "674aef77-64bb-5be9-9abf-cff3f0fb378e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "683e3791-c874-5158-8d10-18c20b047e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-165417" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{3} \\\\  & ={sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{7}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {u}^{8}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78d66bb7-295d-5426-85d5-ecc60dac6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d66bb7-295d-5426-85d5-ecc60dac6869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363597} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a9dd6a1-550f-5ff1-ac0d-0d56b1c23d4f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6b076b-e766-5c8f-a0cd-03e5ed12203c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6b076b-e766-5c8f-a0cd-0