ield/id #uuid "1b6eb381-0284-5836-85ce-d79a42d2b9b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482359e7-082a-504d-bc57-f6163c9f176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "482359e7-082a-504d-bc57-f6163c9f176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363607} :answer-field/id #uuid "61590ccc-fca1-55ef-a95d-40c03c2fae1e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "290de3f9-01bf-5fef-a22f-da08c098a0ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-253323" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-6}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ $n=3,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nSince $n=3,$ we need to compute the first $3-1=2$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\pi )) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2502a117-f0a2-5d25-8266-26db174cee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364063} {:answer/id #uuid "051966b0-a387-52dc-996f-11db19163995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364064} {:answer/id #uuid "63109a39-0802-5e0b-9374-aef3582ed692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2502a117-f0a2-5d25-8266-26db174cee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364063} :answer-field/id #uuid "812da627-3b48-5d0c-9fa5-90a28cca8db4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df08ed8-7546-5403-b5f9-bb8370a55738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364067} {:answer/id #uuid "448bba3a-a057-5a71-8f39-3a480eeb7e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364068} {:answer/id #uuid "07e6a9d8-e0ac-5246-8ad0-bdf8d6396476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448bba3a-a057-5a71-8f39-3a480eeb7e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364068} :answer-field/id #uuid "f938eca9-fd86-5840-a013-9fd0d292ce05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ec8b5c-545d-5b86-9913-1096df572a5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339803" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+B\\cdot g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $B\\in R$ is arbitrary. \n\nSelect functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ that make this statement true.\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP)\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P,Q,$ and $f$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIf the associated homogeneous BVP has nontrivial solutions, then the inhomogeneous BVP has either no solution or infinitely many solutions. \n\nTo find the solution of an inhomogeneous boundary value problem (BVP):\n\n- First, we find the general solution ${y}_{h}(x)$ of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\n- Then, we find a particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the inhomogeneous equation.\n\n- Finally, we apply the boundary conditions to determine the arbitrary constants.\n\nHowever, in this particular case, we can integrate the equation directly. By doing so, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Ax+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that the derivative ${y}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(-2)=2$ gives\n\n$$\n2+A=2\\,⇒\\,A=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0+A & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $B.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the boundary value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+B \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+B(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,g(x)=1.\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note**: The associated homogeneous BVP\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the inhomogeneous BVP has either infinitely many solutions or no solution. Since we have found a solution that contains an arbitrary constant and satisfies both boundary conditions, the BVP has infinitely many solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2548572d-1828-5c15-9c24-9f69c26499fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364332} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf16357-ff08-50aa-8d48-287a20aa338c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364333} {:answer/id #uuid "fa312d6c-985e-5953-8527-699e0ddc3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364334} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf0fdd7-7278-5aa7-85cf-08123b8757dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364335} {:answer/id #uuid "33458f8c-ced5-59e6-a323-8288679d085f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33458f8c-ced5-59e6-a323-8288679d085f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364336} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18620e4-b83a-5036-9cc0-164c3885d9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55c20ddf-7f41-5979-9443-af4fb8f03ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73342" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+2={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(5+4f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}+4(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =5(1-(-2))+10 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2d5a83-8718-5bdf-8eb3-db892e2ab5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438532} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaaea36-3739-5e8a-a1ed-efc15f9bc00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2d5a83-8718-5bdf-8eb3-db892e2ab5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438532} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efd7542-c77b-5d4d-8f60-5468e0925423" :answer-field/key "The system has no solution" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "763f0363-fe65-5380-acc8-6965de771643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438534} {:answer/id #uuid "be014b12-0b58-51d7-a7a3-d343e4554293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be014b12-0b58-51d7-a7a3-d343e4554293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438535} :answer-field/id #uuid "1338d41d-dfae-54bb-ada6-b58b1d8cc303" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent independent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48fb77e-36ef-5915-aaab-7df2660d984a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438536} {:answer/id #uuid "84b40478-f69c-539e-aac5-dd183a05b951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f48fb77e-36ef-5915-aaab-7df2660d984a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438536} :answer-field/id #uuid "7051a40c-c2de-5375-95a6-8801b39f67c5" :answer-field/key "The system is consistent dependent" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b0295d-1e16-59cc-a201-696e791e0b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74643" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=8 \\\\ x+y &=-2 \\\\ x+\\frac{1}{2}y &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=-2 \\\\ -y &=14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-14.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-14)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=12.\n$$\n\n \nSo,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent.   \n\nAs a result, only statement “The system is consistent independent” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e48e2f8-a998-591f-85a7-74216c330826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364401} {:answer/id #uuid "86c854f3-9b37-56bf-8ed0-d656aca9cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} {:answer/id #uuid "b16fdea2-eae9-5267-a3ec-033d8b940279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364403} {:answer/id #uuid "828d078c-1cec-5c53-b243-49495f238d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364404} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1bb85-3d86-5ec5-aae3-1f0c60397c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c854f3-9b37-56bf-8ed0-d656aca9cb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e304ce4-5b90-51ac-8564-dc2bcdec4f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3efc6eb6-1183-58cd-bd1a-3a29f39410eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76614" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -0}{4}-\\frac{0-0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc4fe3ab-834d-54e6-8aa2-1f24a32b9e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364408} {:answer/id #uuid "11e0dcd6-b288-598a-ba1c-229ef1490ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364409} {:answer/id #uuid "beebdd75-a401-5283-81ff-5aa849295dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364410} {:answer/id #uuid "377ccde4-480f-5a52-99b4-878b8324b114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} {:answer/id #uuid "ef48135e-5181-5bd2-954a-39061d970853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi +2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "377ccde4-480f-5a52-99b4-878b8324b114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a9a65b-096a-5622-a126-8ea061e266fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088ac295-32da-54ad-8397-ade18f5cdf50" :question/math-academy-id "q-76683" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le 0.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49adc8e9-1dc5-5cb5-9175-f91e269efe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} {:answer/id #uuid "e94aff50-1cf2-58b3-85e2-e76471a133a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364416} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdf46cb-8dea-538a-974a-83bf0db567f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364417} {:answer/id #uuid "119dacc9-0e3b-5285-87d0-21d3cc26e860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364418} {:answer/id #uuid "bddd3fe2-39af-5dc8-a534-e22068d4a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49adc8e9-1dc5-5cb5-9175-f91e269efe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} :answer-field/id #uuid "7944764b-8f2e-517b-9311-deccaafc6156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13c81ebd-eba4-55aa-a40f-bfa1b49cc9df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76763" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$ along the polar curve $r=4$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{3}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{3}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}256{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,024{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =-1\\,024{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-1\\,024\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-256((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =-256(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =192\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e36d9c-e9a2-5446-ad66-2c509425fb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364622} {:answer/id #uuid "6838046c-6bb6-5382-aff4-79052bb83389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364623} {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} {:answer/id #uuid "0e78a432-6e13-5220-8bcd-195f0ec965d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364625} {:answer/id #uuid "eac12345-022f-5cbe-aa4a-767d1ed83136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66729efb-cb03-5e0d-975f-24a9597e8440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364624} :answer-field/id #uuid "736f5b90-2f10-5a15-8216-cf8f1586fc1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c7952d1-1f1c-50a2-a342-5271912d43de" :question/math-academy-id "q-96033" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{6}{18})\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3\\sqrt{2})=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7983aa7b-5216-54f6-b050-58815a2ab40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364629} {:answer/id #uuid "d666a00e-b87c-596f-ab51-91aea6a4472b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364630} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e175c7-d060-594c-a3a4-0d00100f671b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364631} {:answer/id #uuid "cfba2c96-3a0e-57ea-9a97-ef324f21b60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364632} {:answer/id #uuid "751bc3a2-968b-51b3-9ba2-0fdee249461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "751bc3a2-968b-51b3-9ba2-0fdee249461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364633} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f87d5ad-b6ae-5eab-a689-b6a81915da03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36d5df8c-7b50-5acf-8853-a8f4a4bedaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-96034" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544ecd2d-bde7-5531-b6ed-1f7d10a3f9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364751} {:answer/id #uuid "cab4f01d-178a-546b-bd23-3fe90d910fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364752} {:answer/id #uuid "71a1d39e-5367-5159-b5e6-12901992c3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364753} {:answer/id #uuid "dc508202-a859-5fdd-a4a8-38f77d73654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364754} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d064ef-4a9d-55d2-9e65-78879a8a071e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc508202-a859-5fdd-a4a8-38f77d73654b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364754} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf4dec28-6ad5-5a64-90ef-6978e1019e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b0f3d46-2a1f-5dae-8237-79287f94cce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109331" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{y-3}$ that lies between the lines $y=3$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4}.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y-3})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{y-3}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{4y-11}{4(y-3)}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=4y-11.$ Then, $\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$y$** | $3$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{15/4}\\sqrt{4y-11}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "985e3977-3e44-511f-9f39-9e2d482ed5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364758} {:answer/id #uuid "fabdc79a-55e7-5166-93ca-43d3d190fd99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364759} {:answer/id #uuid "04ca053c-9deb-5648-a23c-c40e6457e67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{1+{(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364760} {:answer/id #uuid "4bec0b3c-eecc-5d40-85bf-665176173794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364761} {:answer/id #uuid "7170c244-4e73-5215-bdf5-a6177e90d079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "985e3977-3e44-511f-9f39-9e2d482ed5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364758} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf3c163-585a-58e8-bdd3-011ef184838b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f152892-0c57-54cf-824f-ee3392d9b983" :question/math-academy-id "q-109425" :question/problem "![](images/21/21b55135fd1fbd0c3120b19ee6c4688dea2d8b13ccfe842e95262c87fb0bf6e0.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ -x({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x)=-3{x}^{2}+8x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2}+8x-4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aeba58e-2480-5903-9203-c20e42f54324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=37.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364889} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0694e3-2d64-5b6e-8fdf-99e203f46da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364890} {:answer/id #uuid "145e16e0-16a5-5047-a144-2563881addf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364891} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd66380-ff8b-5bf8-a8b5-4ec3bda201de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=24.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364892} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f848b-1702-55cc-8d51-da43e9b161cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a0694e3-2d64-5b6e-8fdf-99e203f46da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364890} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2fe126-317a-5c9d-9ee4-ef8f3e825381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b21641d-ae30-55b8-ba2d-d86f82ac4d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120784" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=15-3t,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=14\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 15-3t & =0 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(15-3t) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(5)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (15-3t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=14.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(1)-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+C & =14 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}-1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+15t-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(5)=\\frac{1}{2}+15(5)-\\frac{3}{2}(5)^{2}=38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2a94f5e-8e68-549e-a8ed-83dc42cea847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-4x+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364922} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd73d-dd4f-5970-ad42-69fec767d6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\sqrt{5-4x+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364923} {:answer/id #uuid "ead93f82-75e9-5fdd-b570-be82cb5d48e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364924} {:answer/id #uuid "b85abca9-7ac1-5deb-9121-e5e5d0b99371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364925} {:answer/id #uuid "97dabfcf-1d3b-5c66-893e-01e7a3df3a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85abca9-7ac1-5deb-9121-e5e5d0b99371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364925} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee10a059-aa95-593d-a0f7-1d56ebfcbda6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3bc1fc15-2cf1-50bc-bcb4-3171e2123e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126714" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b69c23833e64df8fa1c938bb87b6cfcf8a67018fb1af73424d26e64e49ee4f5.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=2x-{x}^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-{x}^{2})=2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+{(2-2x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8109b0a-084e-505e-9677-6cf31816c39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364929} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3b6592-41e1-5b53-94ef-adc717ecca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364930} {:answer/id #uuid "867fe616-bbda-594a-bfee-71689e98ac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364931} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cb1890-ceb3-525c-9a55-9c6ad9e148bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364932} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ca7ae-4d0c-52bf-ab6d-6e2d8df75e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7cb1890-ceb3-525c-9a55-9c6ad9e148bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364932} :answer-field/id #uuid "14229d88-ad4c-5463-b8a3-92a65fb233c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6bfdd4b-1abf-5cab-8f44-31d764fee083" :question/math-academy-id "q-126730" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{1-2y}$ that lies between the lines $y=-3$ and $y=0.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{1-2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2\\sqrt{1-2y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-2y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-2y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-2y}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2y}{1-2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\sqrt{1-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi {[-\\frac{2}{3}(1-y)^{3/2}]}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi (-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{28\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b110c6-9fd5-576d-9f5b-275ce0d70db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364943} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed75cab-f2d8-58b4-8474-83308e971b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364944} {:answer/id #uuid "1126d603-8ddc-50e0-b1c7-0654ca022b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364945} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad74be8-da86-55d8-b8a1-77a8d96f3d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364946} {:answer/id #uuid "5aaacd48-c1e7-5856-9575-7beafa4902dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ed75cab-f2d8-58b4-8474-83308e971b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364944} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea2b633-2caf-5b5f-8fa5-c0931244ee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5b234d3-95b5-5e8d-a6f9-df8675102205" :question/math-academy-id "q-127298" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=0$, given that  $p(0)=2\\,i+j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(0)=i-2\\,j-2\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,p(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}\\,{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(2\\,i+j-k)+(\\,i-2\\,j-2k) \\\\  & =\\,i-2\\,j-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (i-2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is  $T(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3}\\,j-\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6838b8cf-fec7-51b2-8373-943cee569878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365276} {:answer/id #uuid "8205c3ac-78de-533c-ae96-ff65f5828af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365277} {:answer/id #uuid "c11907f7-3548-559e-a061-4bcaf9c1b34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365278} {:answer/id #uuid "a7869954-b653-5a50-9765-f8085d861bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365279} {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bec3e13-4258-5707-beaf-f4fdea05190d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3028a6b-dad4-502a-85ce-856d7efdc2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "346646b9-04ff-58cb-a1cc-6c9b42073112" :question/math-academy-id "q-163551" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m$ and the particle has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\pi \\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b396b80995e34fe348b3d059737b1eec3d4ce5a9d2e9138a8e7d4ed5c0945dc0.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=\\pi \\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =8-k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\ 32 & =8+k \\\\ k & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5eca-1b90-5fe4-9c4b-927d2a442f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365352} {:answer/id #uuid "58cd2a77-5515-5752-ad84-4537d236a6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365353} {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} {:answer/id #uuid "714cdf7d-69ae-5343-9e54-3467a962889f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365355} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8579b3-2d8a-5067-90b8-819ebd380eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31af2651-f16b-59aa-957f-024743b9371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365354} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a20e2d-0bf7-5a2c-9004-99c10ddffbbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7407b0b-36b6-5f0d-b3ce-2c90d7096c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-163573" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $1\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ find the value of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cdb88c0c679ca9325df42c5c8c4feaea6ce6c0cf02655410158860d7b03d54dc.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(4x-2)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-(4x-2).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,m/s$ when $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{2})+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2x \\\\ 2x(1-x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution of this equation for $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}$ is $x=1\\,m.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfc85ec-dfe5-55f8-8344-1565d1cc6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bfc85ec-dfe5-55f8-8344-1565d1cc6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365596} :answer-field/id #uuid "d38da886-33fc-549f-aacd-376765d1cdee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b80bca0b-0d46-5280-a67f-47b4201c0da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80bca0b-0d46-5280-a67f-47b4201c0da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365598} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc902c79-b3dd-53ba-8f15-9e0b419740ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88f28c66-d759-5b8d-b8f7-f31f33a24401" :question/math-academy-id "q-328436" :question/problem "The center of the hyperbola $9{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-54x=-56$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}-54x & =-56 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-54x\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9[{x}^{2}-6x]\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9[(x-3)^{2}-9]\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}-81\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =-56 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}\\,-\\,4{y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}}\\,-\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,3,0\\,).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a799a4-fcba-57a2-93a4-8f0c7b4e0152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4a799a4-fcba-57a2-93a4-8f0c7b4e0152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365642} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d6ac1d-7328-5250-958d-5f67f25922a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485d41b3-ae2a-558b-94dc-d65f80b383c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485d41b3-ae2a-558b-94dc-d65f80b383c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365644} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ad1fbe-7ae7-5ce2-a347-163e4a026338" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2a6942-d753-5843-937a-ae3bf9388a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333708" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′}=6x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{4})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4y{y}^{′} & =6x \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6x+4y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{6x+4y{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{6x+4yz}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈0$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})=z(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a222e3-c0cd-51ae-8951-9d144bb362ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.4102\\times {10}^{17}}{r}+(1.464\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365719} {:answer/id #uuid "b42abe59-c840-5f25-8b3c-3cb37dd414c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{8.6204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365720} {:answer/id #uuid "b22fe792-9122-5033-97c9-a74562db32b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{17}}{{r}^{2}}+(1.464\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365721} {:answer/id #uuid "31d6b42f-5c6f-5ad6-9736-2da6165a7ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}-(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365722} {:answer/id #uuid "7571de97-cc39-59d7-88f0-5f746a4f0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7571de97-cc39-59d7-88f0-5f746a4f0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365723} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ced8e56-b5e1-5166-8485-98b96307c88d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccbff46-9435-57a4-ae53-244098c20951" :question/math-academy-id "q-336081" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(1.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=11.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(7.4\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(1.7\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=11.8,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(7.4\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(7.4\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 11.8\\cdot (1.7\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23.6\\cdot (2.89\\times {10}^{14})}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(40.12\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{68.204\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(54.76\\times {10}^{8})-(4.012\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+((54.76-4.012)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(50.748\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6.8204\\times {10}^{15}}{r}+(5.0748\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4725da3c-09bc-5907-8640-bf7c25de282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+t}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365726} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cb9fd6-1ec7-5ce0-b76b-cd19af257096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+t}⟨1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365727} {:answer/id #uuid "8edde89a-7624-55c8-a7af-35358692a3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365728} {:answer/id #uuid "15b9cb2b-f6cc-53ec-8fd9-5695161b485f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365729} {:answer/id #uuid "16ab3056-45e3-59ca-92b3-886c3dc6ed0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ab3056-45e3-59ca-92b3-886c3dc6ed0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365730} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ab9041-3f6f-5fe9-9218-b6f7268e04a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999eae56-9014-5e14-ac70-9c3dd9daada7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33627" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the unit tangent vector to the curve\n $r(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (0,\\infty )?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t),\\,z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+(1)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+1+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Finally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,1,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}⟨-1,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d72036d-f499-5da5-84bb-887df07e964a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.25\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(1.44\\times {10}^{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365749} {:answer/id #uuid "4dee8621-fb18-5939-921f-54b29640ca54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.25\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365750} {:answer/id #uuid "b02f327e-80bb-5bab-8017-7218b4e3cdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365751} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4a8447-1573-5ded-a399-9e542be9cd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{{r}^{2}}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365752} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cb986e-b3bd-5b2a-9628-5d2ddb111aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02f327e-80bb-5bab-8017-7218b4e3cdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365751} :answer-field/id #uuid "54ddeb15-24ce-5063-8338-21a9e8f7ab82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cf83604-10d1-5208-b75e-d8b052cb3eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336343" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(5\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(1.2\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(5\\times {10}^{8}),$\n$g=9,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(1.2\\times {10}^{5}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 9\\cdot (5\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{r}+(1.2\\times {10}^{5})^{2}-2\\cdot 9\\cdot (5\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{450\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(1.44\\times {10}^{10})-(9\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(14.4\\times {10}^{9})-(9\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+((14.4-9)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(5.4\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5c4875-9405-5f06-9640-986c7cb8c624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365756} {:answer/id #uuid "c952625b-a5ef-51b2-9fcc-8cf623dd17c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}+(3.6\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365757} {:answer/id #uuid "1e83eb17-5467-5a9e-a47d-b2c1302485dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365758} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d69858-66db-51a3-be7e-68960832e422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{0.8\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(3.6\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365759} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ff6cd7-0135-58fe-a92d-367df34e725a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{0.8\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e83eb17-5467-5a9e-a47d-b2c1302485dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365758} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61a3586-e160-5c0c-9ac7-bb9ff2f7f999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40b26c37-4285-5c4a-a408-42bee90acd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-336344" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(2\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=20\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(6\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(2\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=20,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(6\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 20\\cdot (2\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(6\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 20\\cdot (2\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{160\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(36\\times {10}^{8})-(80\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(3.6\\times {10}^{9})-(0.8\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+((3.6-0.8)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(2.8\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdf13ef-7092-5823-b962-52c5b08a1f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdf13ef-7092-5823-b962-52c5b08a1f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365763} :answer-field/id #uuid "f94d3759-a44a-5de9-adfc-871f4e6acb3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365762} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036f3fb2-564d-50d2-8910-431714bb2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036f3fb2-564d-50d2-8910-431714bb2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365765} :answer-field/id #uuid "7501eb5d-92fd-54ba-9cf5-acf3ae11bbe8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8b28ec-b785-5775-9854-bc7fe8ba4126" :question/math-academy-id "q-336373" :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(4.2\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=5.6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object, \n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate.\n\n${v}_{e}≈($\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r\\to \\infty ,$ the expression $\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\n{v}_{e}=\\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(4.2\\times {10}^{8})$ and $g=5.6,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{8})\\cdot 5.6} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 4.2\\cdot 5.6)\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{47.04}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & ≈(6.86\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c44db92-d96d-57c9-a934-81a275972da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c44db92-d96d-57c9-a934-81a275972da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365768} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8fb2897-12de-5864-9088-ea47c31c14b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a71b979e-d62d-529b-a738-fdae3e60fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a71b979e-d62d-529b-a738-fdae3e60fda4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365770} :answer-field/id #uuid "9af8d35a-6232-51b9-81d8-b3162439889d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e4b410d-908b-51d6-be88-dbb25fbad4d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336375" :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(4.9\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=11\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object, \n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate.\n\n${v}_{e}≈($\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r\\to \\infty ,$ the expression $\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\n{v}_{e}=\\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(4.9\\times {10}^{6})$ and $g=11,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (4.9\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot 11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 4.9\\cdot 11)\\times {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{107.8}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{6}} \\\\  & ≈(1.04\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24009c93-560f-5227-9499-a47744be5ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24009c93-560f-5227-9499-a47744be5ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{10}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98a0cd0-f327-525c-9988-1ee5db155fbb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365772} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0912ff-2149-504c-b9e6-69d3095fec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident