6045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5063e44-75c8-5c3b-a8e0-5b3e00221500" :question/math-academy-id "q-336405" :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(5.8\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $2.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object.\n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate.\n\n${v}_{e}≈($\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r\\to \\infty ,$ the expression $\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\n{v}_{e}=\\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(5.8\\times {10}^{5})$ and $g=2.4,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (5.8\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot 2.4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 5.8\\cdot 2.4)\\times {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27.84\\times {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.784\\times {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2.784}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{6}} \\\\  & ≈(1.67\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6418cc68-5c14-5b79-a76b-bd6a187118df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22t-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6418cc68-5c14-5b79-a76b-bd6a187118df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22t-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802} :answer-field/id #uuid "108a4f0f-4662-57f5-8305-95c9666f98fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa173e6f-437b-589d-80f8-b753b282ebf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337219" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})\\cdot (1\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t{e}^{-2t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12(t-1)-2(2t+1) \\\\ 24(t-1)-(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8t-14 \\\\ 22t-25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66469292-e41e-5a67-99ec-df0aaf68e52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365847} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9e670f-d8e8-5322-a37e-d005d5880519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365848} {:answer/id #uuid "2e190353-6c36-5b47-b68b-ec17b7a1fb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365849} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc11bbf-bbcb-5a34-837a-3d016c80397b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{10}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365850} {:answer/id #uuid "5579f3bf-d084-5af4-871e-a3e6c59d7a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dc11bbf-bbcb-5a34-837a-3d016c80397b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{10}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365850} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece40dc2-ebbb-5d5c-85c4-560e1f98be15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5285e5cf-340b-5291-b9cf-26149e7c95bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365853} {:answer/id #uuid "e5916abb-30ce-5c32-b64a-39b5064dba58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365854} {:answer/id #uuid "7b459360-1c09-5442-8c13-a287739ed0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365855} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc2f1f-95c0-5d3b-a4f7-b52addd91bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365856} {:answer/id #uuid "8470a03b-e75b-5d40-aeb7-2fc477611826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{3x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-3x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5916abb-30ce-5c32-b64a-39b5064dba58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-3x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365854} :answer-field/id #uuid "97eefe35-e1f9-5454-952d-780ac1837255" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4441ae7-d431-5bda-8014-05a3add48089" :question/math-academy-id "q-338833" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-20\\lambda {y}^{′}+100{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-20\\lambda m+100{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-10\\lambda )(m-10\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=10\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{10\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{10\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{10\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{10\\lambda x}+10B\\lambda x{e}^{10\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }+\\frac{10}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{5\\lambda }(1+\\frac{10}{3}\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{5\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{10\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b9eadc9-cd9b-51ab-ade7-9f484b76759c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365871} {:answer/id #uuid "26611d1c-86f1-50c1-9a9a-b481427f5608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365872} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a1a1f-df5e-5870-8e0b-e2f77c054017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365873} {:answer/id #uuid "a24d8b9c-1c7d-5527-abec-031b9aeb91cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365874} {:answer/id #uuid "9d21c9a3-6fb6-5a29-8ef7-93804f21d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d21c9a3-6fb6-5a29-8ef7-93804f21d5a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365875} :answer-field/id #uuid "90babee2-f98c-50aa-ba37-d79ed5ae8e0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd198d63-470c-5935-90ca-c611184e6694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365877} {:answer/id #uuid "2b64e29c-bcf8-5bd7-bd36-fbfba1f2cb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365878} {:answer/id #uuid "674d5b0f-4a62-527b-8517-5402990dfa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365879} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce5bf32-9a34-5eac-a18c-ba18b98c35e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365880} {:answer/id #uuid "1c81dba1-98d4-54ef-8024-4bbdbb0b0b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365881} {:answer/id #uuid "bbacf00a-7086-5572-b242-d3c6a6a993d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd198d63-470c-5935-90ca-c611184e6694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365877} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ee1ae1-efa8-536b-ac3d-cc965f58c8c3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6852602-1069-51f1-8d48-415f065da706" :question/math-academy-id "q-338912" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(3)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(3)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 6\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\lambda x)+6B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-6A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(3)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(18\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n18\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{36}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6e6ab0-e1a2-5036-a674-a8a7f4eb423c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365929} {:answer/id #uuid "57b6c77a-6893-516a-8f91-0dedf6e9058a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365930} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4895c-f6d4-5497-8a22-e8d6e6d2cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdb14d7-b5e7-5001-bea2-034d9be5ca5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e4895c-f6d4-5497-8a22-e8d6e6d2cefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365931} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77f080d-827b-51df-b338-49ffb15b987a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94df4605-16e7-5b93-975e-109a28027292" :question/math-academy-id "q-343447" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+2{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+2{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B\\sqrt{2}\\pi +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B\\sqrt{2}\\pi  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\sqrt{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(18-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab86f29f-86b9-5139-b7b9-52289787dff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365962} {:answer/id #uuid "13c96fa5-5aad-5a39-9717-f46276309fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365963} {:answer/id #uuid "99149897-b6f0-53e2-8cbd-173ffe25f56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7d6966-abab-5d76-b3f7-3f492dcb3ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99149897-b6f0-53e2-8cbd-173ffe25f56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365964} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e8b6be-52c3-5fb8-87bd-b14fedfa5492" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c618b807-0d3f-5c96-b840-87c2666fd829" :question/math-academy-id "q-344047" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an odd, $6$-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an odd function, its Fourier series consists only of sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm 2\\pi \\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Using $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+0+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ because the particular solution is a sine series.\n\nNext, we use the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we compute the derivative of the total solution (with $A=0$):\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼B(2\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nApplying ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =B(2\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B(2\\pi ) & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{2n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $B$ back into ${y}_{c}=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)$, we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x){\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{3}(36-{n}^{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81fb9a2a-e974-5e21-a24c-b83847d0670c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81fb9a2a-e974-5e21-a24c-b83847d0670c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365986} :answer-field/id #uuid "991e8bfb-d216-5777-8b02-41d6beedd873" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65696180-4576-5e92-bc35-eb4097717b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65696180-4576-5e92-bc35-eb4097717b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365988} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d560f3e-45c6-5297-9dbb-a7f86861cc41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77799534-8c26-5a8c-81b1-59219a3dda8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-354994" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=5-\\frac{1}{2}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 5-\\frac{1}{2}t & =0 \\\\ t & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme values using the second derivative test. The second derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-\\frac{1}{2}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(10)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (5-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(4)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(4)-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{2}+C & =3 \\\\ 20-4+C & =3\\,⟹\\,C=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity is given by $v(t)=5t-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}-13.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(10)=5(10)-\\frac{1}{4}(10)^{2}-13=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=10\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f80921a7-9ef8-51bc-ba03-c610b343031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f80921a7-9ef8-51bc-ba03-c610b343031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365991} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac3bdfa-2313-5db4-aa3b-2fcdafe606ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e86f0a56-8589-5937-b33b-d876d2fcde71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e86f0a56-8589-5937-b33b-d876d2fcde71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365993} :answer-field/id #uuid "9019baa6-75df-546b-b37a-ca7344b0b1e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01deaa82-6ed4-5ec3-bb26-dc6ad125e782" :question/math-academy-id "q-355219" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=7-7{t}^{2},$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 7-7{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 7(1-t)(1+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t⩾0$, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7-7{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-14t \\\\ {v}^{″}(1) & =-14(1) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(1)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (7-7{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2)-\\frac{7}{3}(2)^{3}+C & =1 \\\\ 14-\\frac{56}{3}+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{17}{3}+7t-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(1)=\\frac{17}{3}+7(1)-\\frac{7}{3}(1)^{3}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{31}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f0dba1-7067-5d87-99e6-9cf227b19ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366106} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbc4e23-72cd-5330-af02-7585346a0eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366107} {:answer/id #uuid "c4eb1a09-3f4d-5544-bbe3-aa4a4488f180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366108} {:answer/id #uuid "e6af34c5-cd3c-5f65-9633-120d1ff65e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366109} {:answer/id #uuid "e115b02c-9eb3-5e9c-9adc-f461206a1807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6af34c5-cd3c-5f65-9633-120d1ff65e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366109} :answer-field/id #uuid "b10f82d7-23d8-5faa-bd60-f6f74f7c290a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a318f0c-c590-5820-90e1-5e9d0a54a78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39411" :question/problem "![](images/48/48d76f34becfba9f58658c3c38ba2df0e76ad6ff20b83ffc836e213c4eea4565.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[6]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})dz\\,dy\\,dx.$ The region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[6]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})dz\\,dy\\,dx={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[6]{\\pi },\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86af6e58f4f13e79bb9672cef329572f006e5f4875178ede7908bddbd0dec404.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/83041605fcd79db34f249664b12311a4e5d797c331bcaf1279f03b5b68891b23.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz={y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt[3]{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5a3fc034b713ce5fca98fbd0cca3c454d18e42e0e73eecc7ec18c51a8e7ebb5.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the bottom curve given by $z=\\sqrt[3]{0}=0.$\n\n![](images/54/54fa3b1c3a15391e0a87c15017b07594c4f2cca7fb741cd7480909a64e3d39db.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[6]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2}){[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{1}{3}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+{z}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162d9cfd-034a-5c33-9d0a-906cac7f6fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366134} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e94b1d-482a-5d97-a07a-fd91a8e31af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366135} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6ee2b1-5486-5bb5-8122-ecb889f89cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366136} {:answer/id #uuid "09cba4a0-9f24-50ca-aaaf-b5d7a53af3e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366137} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0db238-7843-5a87-bfad-905b7e4e7ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0db238-7843-5a87-bfad-905b7e4e7ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366138} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bd81a8-fc97-5b1d-aab5-22729b731045" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "140b78de-e11f-5db2-951c-82c8f5734177" :question/math-academy-id "q-41624" :question/problem "$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V,$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector ${v}_{3}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{18}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 6 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{3}{2},$ $c=\\frac{3}{2},$ and $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a01fc108-f471-5f76-a5ee-417c7d24e5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=8{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366186} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6d5d09-c35a-5526-9c7b-a7bd9eb06ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366187} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb1b9a9-8877-55ae-b463-b16761bb04b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366188} {:answer/id #uuid "db1b5398-4dab-5a15-80d8-5b66f4fd867a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366189} {:answer/id #uuid "f75e9fc2-6f9f-5f8d-b317-5038fb18bb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb1b9a9-8877-55ae-b463-b16761bb04b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366188} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c0fc63-5b76-5b1c-b861-02062bd63560" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1f6bedb-2eaf-5948-8752-536128e92201" :question/math-academy-id "q-64954" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t-4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+(-3\\alpha +2\\beta )=t-4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -3\\alpha +2\\beta &=-4\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t-\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{2t-5}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1f7f88-f4d6-55ab-96bb-e95b8529508e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366381} {:answer/id #uuid "761d56bb-9090-5642-9a0c-e14547f22359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366382} {:answer/id #uuid "8c80468f-7fc8-5012-914f-f4ac6b573a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366383} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1145a6-cf7a-5bfb-b5d9-39965f8e4d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366384} {:answer/id #uuid "e440fb99-3dab-5905-b79d-54b15b6644a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a1f7f88-f4d6-55ab-96bb-e95b8529508e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366381} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1b294cd-0690-5b2a-8b79-bfdfd5228580" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbde2955-a5db-5798-a76a-665975736935" :question/math-academy-id "q-81212" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=6,$ $b=\\frac{9}{2},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(6)}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d890c58-af91-51f8-85ea-2bd07fad71ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366408} {:answer/id #uuid "75003568-9821-5daf-9547-5c8d08e948b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=9\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366409} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe9381d-5f8b-5423-8662-414e63ce1e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=12\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366410} {:answer/id #uuid "59ff1ecd-83ff-5776-a575-d84980260b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366411} {:answer/id #uuid "31b0f3bb-1a29-59a1-9bdc-cf6e4a5ef47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=9\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ff1ecd-83ff-5776-a575-d84980260b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366411} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fe62c1-6c0e-5da2-9b35-9e17db8f2d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d63e1e-878a-5c14-84af-3c1645e3dcb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81840" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6-2t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-2t) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6-2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)-(1)^{2}+C & =2 \\\\ 6-1+C & =2\\,⟹\\,C=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=-{t}^{2}+6t-3.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=-(3)^{2}+6(3)-3=6\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4230ad4-7cfc-5d03-bab5-c470ed638956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366415} {:answer/id #uuid "b65c9efc-7c3f-5bfb-817c-61630ff08b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366416} {:answer/id #uuid "63061252-851d-512f-947d-df9b1f834d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366417} {:answer/id #uuid "cefa53ae-b66e-5476-a63b-cd4860f7c92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366418} {:answer/id #uuid "58b00c9b-e358-5249-91e6-9a3e85bd720d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58b00c9b-e358-5249-91e6-9a3e85bd720d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366419} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8329e9-7ccd-5315-91fb-dae2ef3df42f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2bf4373d-fd90-5472-8b82-87ff2c920c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-82049" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time  in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the earliest time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. \n\nTherefore, the extrema of the velocity function, for $t>0,$ are\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},…\n$$\n\nWe now test the extrema values, starting with the least, using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-12\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\nTherefore, the first time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+C & =1 \\\\ 0+C & =1\\,⟹\\,C=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSo the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ is reached first time at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bf3705-350d-591c-b5e3-774879c04b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366422} {:answer/id #uuid "85431788-860f-50e2-a965-1c7466c5f914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366423} {:answer/id #uuid "358a2c45-0561-50d3-8098-e22cfeea88d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366424} {:answer/id #uuid "21706d9b-1079-5114-9592-b5828ac7cbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366425} {:answer/id #uuid "d74b5df7-d508-53c4-b1f7-8dfcab46c255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "358a2c45-0561-50d3-8098-e22cfeea88d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366424} :answer-field/id #uuid "db368684-bf29-5c1c-8ce6-a4b27f0856f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7191c9ef-9aad-5881-a936-bd45ed790f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-82500" :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ a & b & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}x-z \\\\ x+y \\\\ x-y-z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8 & -2 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 2 & -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{1}{4},$ $b=-\\frac{1}{4},$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+1=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f024086-bc4f-50c7-879f-d67d9826ebee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366527} {:answer/id #uuid "d74a082c-4c0c-540e-87e1-35c8ac407170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366528} {:answer/id #uuid "070992ba-70a6-5d23-b7bf-86ba4b7d94b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366529} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc5ae26-bf75-52b0-af4a-500d0d179614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366530} {:answer/id #uuid "37a5b36d-fa96-5ae6-b6ae-d154f060c774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a5b36d-fa96-5ae6-b6ae-d154f060c774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366531} :answer-field/id #uuid "48dad23b-c580-5561-a75c-a5c73aafe50d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dd16f7a-f327-54ce-81b8-5d78600e9324" :question/math-academy-id "q-86851" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=\\frac{1}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab3ef03-ef16-5a68-abca-b6dcbb1377b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366576} {:answer/id #uuid "05593484-c115-5b5b-a903-792a9f39921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366577} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ad9393-eccd-53c3-a462-bfa7e8b3b690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{20}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366578} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa73147-4c74-541b-97e9-b5152e5229e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{20}-\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366579} {:answer/id #uuid "dd803bf5-f6f2-5117-b3e7-4820dca36208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05593484-c115-5b5b-a903-792a9f39921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366577} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f68fbe8-e882-5bd1-a210-7028b835e815" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f850cdc8-9f93-564a-97ba-ba274381f88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94214" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-10y=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-10y=5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{5t}}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0+3\\alpha -10(\\alpha t+\\beta )=5t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-10\\alpha t-10\\beta +3\\alpha =t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-10\\alpha &=5\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -10\\beta +3\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{20},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aff604c-8612-5d59-a83a-b4bff7afa693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366672} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba28056-be96-5bb5-9d00-cdb75f7c223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366673} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4e70e8-049c-5b59-890d-0c8c7f808c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366674} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a55167-1c4d-5728-844c-1cb9a9a9cdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366675} {:answer/id #uuid "01361167-700e-598b-a8fe-1795f3c6300d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a55167-1c4d-5728-844c-1cb9a9a9cdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366675} :answer-field/id #uuid "114fa9c1-9d2d-5e26-8ec3-22bb6c29182d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04f2e807-374a-54f0-9c1c-c36bd677ab53" :question/math-academy-id "q-95705" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050d3072-c838-5e08-b0bc-4d5171d8a819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366734} {:answer/id #uuid "e5183501-b9cd-5561-b6e3-c0b1b0bb5462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366735} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e7a5e-a95d-5547-bc90-7be6e5c15598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366736} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed40f62-9010-5b5c-bf46-6927e9dc6923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366737} {:answer/id #uuid "4d718142-0fc1-5c0e-bd69-6c41ab31ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d718142-0fc1-5c0e-bd69-6c41ab31ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366738} :answer-field/id #uuid "296453d6-3c71-5737-82c3-c5c36630c17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb83c793-f479-5cb9-b03e-05eb6c2cfac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-96075" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=\\frac{35}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{35}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{36}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d35d5b-b6eb-5bb1-ab46-be0009f16832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366933} {:answer/id #uuid "0c13ddbf-2bab-5175-a02e-e65b64f47de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366934} {:answer/id #uuid "caef2243-31b7-5e0b-9c68-9f5d0742f8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366935} {:answer/id #uuid "38ad2a3c-967f-57be-b393-a1375fd573b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366936} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff783ee-c731-5fa2-a53d-8f55a1524c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38ad2a3c-967f-57be-b393-a1375fd573b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366936} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c8e3c2-ad15-5b31-a2d3-b484484091cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3830c470-fe4d-59b8-89c7-dfdbce50cb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109233" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[1-2X]$ if $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For any constants $a$ and $b$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX+b]=a\\text{E}[X]+b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[1-2X] & =\\text{E}[-2X+1] \\\\  & =-2\\text{E}[X]+1 \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df8b0ad-baae-504c-ad7b-ebd37fa1cfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366940} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a771ec-9eca-59f0-bb09-210787cc5fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366941} {:answer/id #uuid "98f6dcf7-8138-5999-b311-cf60d04fa006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366942} {:answer/id #uuid "edb32931-3827-5767-875f-e657062e7c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366943} {:answer/id #uuid "27e5ce3e-76e5-5ae0-9d68-2824761c618a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb32931-3827-5767-875f-e657062e7c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366943} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b1c51c-f128-557b-bcff-233ea69f4ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c481646-7776-51cf-8d09-e1f9bb3f54ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-109235" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[-2X-4]$ if $\\text{E}[X]=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For any constants $a$ and $b$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[aX+b]=a\\text{E}[X]+b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[-2X-4] & =-2\\text{E}[X]-4 \\\\  & =-2(-\\frac{5}{2})-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e30cd3-4b5b-5ff0-a936-e0b989496f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366954} {:answer/id #uuid "28ffd7e1-6c6f-56b4-b8e3-eaafd55a1796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366955} {:answer/id #uuid "93c12745-6f17-5bb2-8a1d-db30f6935ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366956} {:answer/id #uuid "44ded528-7c28-5d95-8580-e1f2a21cfb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366957} {:answer/id #uuid "df29654a-fed9-56f1-818e-c79739693b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07e30cd3-4b5b-5ff0-a936-e0b989496f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366954} :answer-field/id #uuid "11759378-4ff7-5552-9b29-e8ab4b78dc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecee9e02-4922-534a-b756-ef416f580361" :question/math-academy-id "q-109306" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[(5X+2)(5X-2)]$ if $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{3}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the product inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(5X+2)(5X-2)] & =\\text{E}[25{X}^{2}-4] \\\\  & =25\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[-4] \\\\  & =25\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-4 \\\\  & =25(\\frac{3}{5})-4 \\\\  & =15-4 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a10c819-f962-517a-bd28-778bb054c660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367172} {:answer/id #uuid "f8bd6f3a-a1b1-5284-b572-c14440792d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367173} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdd6d29-1b1d-5faa-967d-b7e4790b2d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367174} {:answer/id #uuid "a86ebf85-357d-5b1f-a8a6-d8391e75ebd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367175} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb1fcf1-a588-5a87-b4b4-50ba24deaed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbdd6d29-1b1d-5faa-967d-b7e4790b2d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367174} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7de7570-8cd8-57af-9496-2dce3e12f896" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc544fb2-b29c-5843-ace8-c118d11d787f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115947" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4$ and $x=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ -8-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\n Finally, substituting $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae467fb3-f63f-54bc-88d8-32c99fe9e08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367179} {:answer/id #uuid "bd67acba-1d7d-5904-9fd2-988231e488fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367180} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b4a166-30db-52fa-98df-8a94aafd5f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367181} {:answer/id #uuid "3d267227-97a9-5d1c-9990-ea64e0fab1ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367182} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc32ff-1826-5302-8b38-a4e5f595c725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd67acba-1d7d-5904-9fd2-988231e488fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367180} :answer-field/id #uuid "933d59db-68a6-5d20-bc41-310ebed025cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04fc9383-ba9d-51b2-b7ff-6b5bffaa64a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-115950" :question/problem "Consider the curve $\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}) & =0 \\\\ -8+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{y}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ 2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acac4a85-c84f-5d34-bfe0-21ed8f8c4ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367277} {:answer/id #uuid "f23d3732-2065-57dd-bdfc-3cff32fb2fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367278} {:answer/id #uuid "90c4d89f-267a-5a07-be30-7097bd34770d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367279} {:answer/id #uuid "648438f6-e370-5d5c-b70a-8997050de19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367280} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d21000-77af-55bf-8980-0f65613e47bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d21000-77af-55bf-8980-0f65613e47bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367281} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f66454-506d-5a70-b021-6c3ed065a4e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "568e41c7-85eb-5a4c-a65e-d59bc03eb737" :question/math-academy-id "q-130218" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}||f(s)||,$ if $f(s)=⟨{e}^{s-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-s},\\,\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨{lim}_{s\\to 2}{e}^{s-2},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-s},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{3}{s+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}{e}^{s-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-s} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{3}{s+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{89}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97e6ea88-079d-58fc-a64d-93edf5641731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367312} {:answer/id #uuid "058c0b0f-b3e0-57be-a3c9-7a990be97cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367313} {:answer/id #uuid "5a44425a-f301-557a-95e2-e7e2c518aaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367314} {:answer/id #uuid "793238bb-7ba0-52d8-a073-7b268b261240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367315} {:answer/id #uuid "f252716f-d206-5351-b7cd-7c08ca2f109e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a44425a-f301-557a-95e2-e7e2c518aaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367314} :answer-field/id #uuid "acb41a73-8b4b-5fd4-b327-56cf63e45f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03af07b5-4c80-5378-9b22-977c05cd1f42" :question/math-academy-id "q-130316" :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+t \\\\ {e}^{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}({t}^{2}+t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}{e}^{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)}{t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\ {e}^{-1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1+1)}{(-1)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}2 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}\\frac{1-2t}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1) \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{1-2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to \\,-1}g(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0(0)+1(2)+(-1)(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c464ae52-c792-5966-9d49-a2e4829b9b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367438} {:answer/id #uuid "60181620-1848-5691-9b01-0836bdae2569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367439} {:answer/id #uuid "1ffce98a-3785-54ef-a67a-a79256e5e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367440} {:answer/id #uuid "61d68627-501b-5d8d-a22c-84fdf7f0c3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367441} {:answer/id #uuid "5a336a5d-1d0b-5110-8865-6d48c421f50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ffce98a-3785-54ef-a67a-a79256e5e528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac10a081-d7a4-5b23-9a50-e6500fcd48c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e0db89b-f0c6-50f0-a4df-0e579944e4eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-137033" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\\,{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-4b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-\\frac{3}{2})+2{x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-4b+c=-2-4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+0=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faba9b8c-2db9-5102-aa65-00748558a463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367445} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d0ce5d-e84e-57e4-8a47-bf9cb06a4a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367446} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1f8972-dbc3-5adb-b2ee-427452d50a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367447} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3fff1f-5172-5721-9989-85b34c09b139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367448} {:answer/id #uuid "fb047e4f-69c3-5551-ba4d-0963ccacfe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1f8972-dbc3-5adb-b2ee-427452d50a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367447} :answer-field/id #uuid "778fca66-9171-578b-a82f-9dd5127d9503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9b18fd2-40c6-532b-a289-0305c371077f" :question/math-academy-id "q-137090" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-4a+\\frac{b}{c}=-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2e8bc30-41c9-56c8-a5b2-e047a033dedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367465} {:answer/id #uuid "b854c123-c85d-5e04-a948-bd8a101d0416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367466} {:answer/id #uuid "14edfcb2-c8de-5378-b4d9-078fcd77b9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} {:answer/id #uuid "8922920d-3e40-5b79-b054-c8ad34ddd743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367468} {:answer/id #uuid "f50415c4-c627-581d-942c-cb7597564764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14edfcb2-c8de-5378-b4d9-078fcd77b9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c091cf5-1213-5f71-95d4-647a3b748c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cc47049-1d82-526c-84a3-b9c51fd53a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-139026" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{-t},\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=-2$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t})=-2{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{-t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfc3282-f67f-5c37-ac20-e055804cec92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367472} {:answer/id #uuid "5da30080-28c4-5744-8ac6-43e6e2a96e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4)\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367473} {:answer/id #uuid "936e31ff-aad2-5671-b08c-0fa686997e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t)\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367474} {:answer/id #uuid "7d95b191-aa8b-5d93-9bb0-e81b8a9878a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367475} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eba06b-dd48-5f6b-83d8-0809f2454d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bfc3282-f67f-5c37-ac20-e055804cec92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367472} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f020de9-ae3e-53bd-98e8-98a1724485d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48e4ffbf-2c4f-5623-a301-c10de09f0525" :question/math-academy-id "q-139028" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2}+4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-{t}^{2})=-2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4t)=2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{{(-2t)}^{2}+{(2t+4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+16t+16}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+16t+16}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{16(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\cdot 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c28d0505-608b-54b4-9b41-4432c9f6d4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{10t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367479} {:answer/id #uuid "660b58be-797f-55e4-b862-862f1118e66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t}\\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367480} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5554e6-7c6b-504f-8963-8de0515bfbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367481} {:answer/id #uuid "b522f653-6291-54f2-ba12-56068ef449b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367482} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3cf73a-1131-5a64-bf88-e092f7057397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{{e}^{10t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b522f653-6291-54f2-ba12-56068ef449b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367482} :answer-field/id #uuid "b118e46c-6411-5f99-abdf-ecc41ee73bbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06c08f7d-1989-5d8b-9502-e2a022d8abd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139030" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{5t},\\,y=5{e}^{2t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{5t})=10{e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{e}^{2t})=10{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{5t}\\sqrt{{(10{e}^{5t})}^{2}+{(10{e}^{2t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\sqrt{100{e}^{10t}+100{e}^{4t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\sqrt{100{e}^{4t}({e}^{6t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot \\sqrt{100{e}^{4t}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{5t}\\cdot 10{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\sqrt{{e}^{6t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3602d4-7774-5286-bdb0-42626915479b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367486} {:answer/id #uuid "a86646f6-0aea-5838-9281-711fc0aaab24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367487} {:answer/id #uuid "d16f8a6b-65f3-5d35-965b-7c8661d15a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367488} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2cc10f-e116-5a70-8e54-83885a24828a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367489} {:answer/id #uuid "a0aedead-4663-537d-a1e2-ed4fd155d953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d16f8a6b-65f3-5d35-965b-7c8661d15a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367488} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7670fb6-77b3-5fc1-8438-31c45735ec36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c400062-b704-53b2-a5d1-f71fc8c9b881" :question/math-academy-id "q-139103" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is subject to the external forces \n\n$$\n{F}_{1}=⟨2,\\,4{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩,\\,{F}_{2}=⟨8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-2,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{1}$ and ${F}_{2}$ are measured in Newtons and $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the velocity of $P$ equals $⟨0,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nThe resultant force $F$ acting on $P$ equals the sum of the forces acting on $P.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & ={F}_{1}+{F}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,4{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩+⟨8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-2,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-2,\\,4{sin}^{3}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy Newton's second law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{F}{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ⟨8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the velocity vector $v,$ we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\int a\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a (vector) constant of integration. \n\nWe can solve for $C$ by substituting $v=⟨0,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{m/s}$ and $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0,\\,-1⟩ & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩+C \\\\ ⟨0,\\,-1⟩ & =⟨-1,\\,-1⟩+C \\\\ C & =⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally  the velocity at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d0c4ec-499e-5239-9cae-00449b99882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{4},\\,16⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367500} {:answer/id #uuid "dec45b9f-20f4-5d84-9467-60c56150f7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,\\,36⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367501} {:answer/id #uuid "25a36e99-63a8-580d-91bc-c17cb3e125c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,\\,48⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367502} {:answer/id #uuid "65d39718-0b5a-5150-99ff-11dcf7cf35af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,\\,36⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367503} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6406e8-9971-5ec1-9306-9f63e3ce7818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,\\,16⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65d39718-0b5a-5150-99ff-11dcf7cf35af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,\\,36⟩\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367503} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e1330e4-5500-5514-8e4c-314b718aaed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8aa9178-f900-5da3-a252-6f4b6d998e3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139106" :question/problem "The velocity $v,$ in meters per second, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{12}{t},\\,{t}^{3}-1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{12}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{12}{{t}^{2}},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{12}{{t}^{2}},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{36}{{t}^{2}},\\,9{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $t=2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =⟨-\\frac{36}{{2}^{2}},\\,9(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-9,\\,36⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the resultant force acting on $P$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ equals $⟨-9,\\,36⟩\\,\\text{N}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeda1f60-14bd-5df8-9352-7d70f966c123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367535} {:answer/id #uuid "424d08cc-81a8-5913-94b1-2308c43428bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367536} {:answer/id #uuid "59dff0c1-7213-5a78-a3c0-0d5174c6f388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367537} {:answer/id #uuid "36da3b7f-06d7-56b4-bf97-2a29cc8c9611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367538} {:answer/id #uuid "677a35f7-cfe9-5ab3-97c1-3705761074b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aeda1f60-14bd-5df8-9352-7d70f966c123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367535} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f0c119-2b79-52e1-972d-76ad0d32928e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4641d84-2178-58cb-b097-44d8dc2cab30" :question/math-academy-id "q-139123" :question/problem "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton." :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot ⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) & =⟨-20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10,\\,15\\sqrt{3}-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})| & =\\sqrt{(10)^{2}+{(15\\sqrt{3}-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈23\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b416a21d-f860-5ddd-9887-3909cbcfc268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367605} {:answer/id #uuid "84ff0b6d-4bc8-58b4-8c1f-7733a5d10ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367606} {:answer/id #uuid "8e61b06f-5796-5daf-a560-740f317dd6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367607} {:answer/id #uuid "52c4db08-6d75-5662-ba65-98e14935cb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367608} {:answer/id #uuid "665ef108-98da-5b42-b5b6-51f12caa5073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84ff0b6d-4bc8-58b4-8c1f-7733a5d10ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367606} :answer-field/id #uuid "7906d722-793f-554d-8af3-30e86eebaf11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c948087e-b394-5663-8b66-c2d6a56d25e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-14451" :question/problem "$\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce484f3-4e5e-550d-818e-f1c78bea979f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367612} {:answer/id #uuid "cedb54b5-de63-5342-98e6-7ed2532c1269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{25+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367613} {:answer/id #uuid "de5e7684-e5e2-5351-ba6d-0d66b899a3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367614} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa2495d-019a-51d8-b8dc-b74be91a15a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367615} {:answer/id #uuid "e6cc5106-cf6c-55a2-bc2e-ed2e5dcfcf7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{24+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ce484f3-4e5e-550d-818e-f1c78bea979f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6af1ed0a-0d03-5e1e-9010-7e07caafd4a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3b6297b-f8d7-5d66-a20a-f484bbbfcbcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-15141" :question/problem "![](images/18/18a6f3e8c2cb509bcdc576a453d0b351caf8193039a8ef9905522be8feabdfac.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(5+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{25+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{25+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+1+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{26+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+24{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10df0bfd-e2a3-5162-ac9c-0c4fc5dd2f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367619} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb690da-cc23-5efd-8b15-76952b5da6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367620} {:answer/id #uuid "77336847-9826-5b88-bbb4-be494b9f81f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367621} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5689ff-8d50-5c68-ab76-395a34693680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367622} {:answer/id #uuid "120c7391-b27d-5053-9e08-1a4ca0f14d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "120c7391-b27d-5053-9e08-1a4ca0f14d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367623} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dda31f-8c48-5a17-810b-de382fd1780a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07a0d06e-e036-5f02-87ab-6bc4e25013e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-156780" :question/problem "Consider the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(2\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ and $y=\\sqrt{3},$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (\\frac{2(2\\sqrt{3})}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2(\\sqrt{3})) & =0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54bfec69-3a74-59c2-9b16-76ba5edbf80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367633} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c944c9-6a99-5e2b-91f7-225f93752b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367634} {:answer/id #uuid "0f055c4c-b56a-566f-9616-ed5427fb6672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367635} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eeb063-a999-56f3-bb0c-234a77302f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367636} {:answer/id #uuid "c040ac86-6e5f-55ea-bfed-10fe8ce74e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c040ac86-6e5f-55ea-bfed-10fe8ce74e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367637} :answer-field/id #uuid "938122b4-fa51-5ebb-a493-63e2b5103d0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a8e6933-d4ad-55a4-98f8-8b8ced5e96c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-156959" :question/problem "Consider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}=-8.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{y}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}$ and $x=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ 0-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cec91b9-efc5-55c0-87dd-05c16ecc997d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367654} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7e099-d651-53a3-8fdf-3643e3987a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367655} {:answer/id #uuid "7074012e-14d8-5135-b6f4-c02aec073201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367656} {:answer/id #uuid "f629da4d-bb91-5bc0-af40-6ca8dbc966fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367657} {:answer/id #uuid "122d5375-583a-5e9f-8e0f-7a9b08e2f234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "122d5375-583a-5e9f-8e0f-7a9b08e2f234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367658} :answer-field/id #uuid "49abf4ab-b58f-5097-9149-6642297d1320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b3524d7-7028-5bad-b5ef-15195604d5b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157163" :question/problem "Consider the section of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the circle at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1)+(\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0. \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,y}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the ellipse. \n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78256e55-f9c0-5f25-9bce-75879edc9fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367785} {:answer/id #uuid "ba371be4-a3c4-507f-8f66-4f2055137231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367786} {:answer/id #uuid "f4193f0d-676e-5937-aff3-0523d50d7633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367787} {:answer/id #uuid "14823d05-96e6-5666-b344-a9dccb8d3e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367788} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5b3af7-e5e3-5a46-ba00-2dda1f55acee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba371be4-a3c4-507f-8f66-4f2055137231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367786} :answer-field/id #uuid "c371aaec-d4e5-5c39-a2b8-4eb96f2445c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d94cbd-ee2b-5982-acfb-18c8cc38370a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163073" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa2eb46-7da9-5a93-ab46-905177cc8d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367792} {:answer/id #uuid "20e67f4c-0912-57d9-b100-79cfe229487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367793} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d766a7-f722-54d5-a4bd-8afd2c4dbcfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367794} {:answer/id #uuid "c70eb82d-5858-5ea5-af10-6241b5f04e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367795} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4cae0c-20a3-51e2-90c3-75d418d9f057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c70eb82d-5858-5ea5-af10-6241b5f04e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367795} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db7e5b9-cbc0-5e13-8675-3951d7667a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08e9a1c4-09e3-536e-9e2b-e51736b93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-163074" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{xy+1},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy+1})=-\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{(xy+1)^{2}} & -\\frac{x}{(xy+1)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-\\frac{1}{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4})+(-\\frac{1}{4})=0.\n$$"}]
